little bit about what

00:16:14.335 --> 00:16:15.620
deployment profiles do for us?

00:16:15.620 --> 00:16:17.098
SUJIT KAMIREDDY: Sure.

00:16:17.098 --> 00:16:18.890
In the real world, an
application typically

00:16:18.890 --> 00:16:20.510
results in multiple
environments,

00:16:20.510 --> 00:16:23.848
such as dev, test,
staging, prod, et cetera.

00:16:23.848 --> 00:16:26.390
You may want to build and deploy
your application differently

00:16:26.390 --> 00:16:27.920
based on the environment.

00:16:27.920 --> 00:16:30.580
For example, in dev
environment, you

00:16:30.580 --> 00:16:33.650
may want to use Docker CLI to
build your application and use

00:16:33.650 --> 00:16:35.120
kubectl to deploy
the application

00:16:35.120 --> 00:16:38.792
to your local cluster, such as
Minikube or Docker for desktop.

00:16:38.792 --> 00:16:40.250
And in staging
environment, you may

00:16:40.250 --> 00:16:42.620
want to use Google
Cloud Build to build you

00:16:42.620 --> 00:16:44.990
application and use customize
to deploy the application

00:16:44.990 --> 00:16:46.940
to a remote GKE cluster.

00:16:46.940 --> 00:16:49.132
Deployment profiles
help us do this.

00:16:49.132 --> 00:16:51.590
For each of the environment
you would like your application

00:16:51.590 --> 00:16:53.300
to be supported
in, you will just

00:16:53.300 --> 00:16:55.010
have to create a
deployment profile

00:16:55.010 --> 00:16:57.290
and let the Cloud Code
take care of the rest.

00:16:57.290 --> 00:16:59.540
So now, we'll go ahead and
create our first deployment

00:16:59.540 --> 00:17:02.600
profile to work against
the local environment.

00:17:02.600 --> 00:17:04.210
And for that, we'll
click as here.

00:17:04.210 --> 00:17:06.530
And now, it has
analyzed the application

00:17:06.530 --> 00:17:09.440
and prompting us to provide a
mapping between the image names

00:17:09.440 --> 00:17:11.420
in the Kubernetes
manifest and the Docker

00:17:11.420 --> 00:17:13.140
files in this application.

00:17:13.140 --> 00:17:15.950
So why do we need to
provide this mapping?

00:17:15.950 --> 00:17:18.260
Usually, in order to
deploy an application,

00:17:18.260 --> 00:17:20.020
we'll have to build
out the Docker files

00:17:20.020 --> 00:17:24.560
in the application, tag them,
push them to a repository,

00:17:24.560 --> 00:17:28.089
and then update our Kubernetes
manifest with the image paths.

00:17:28.089 --> 00:17:30.080
The deploy process is
asking for this mapping,

00:17:30.080 --> 00:17:33.120
so it can do all of these
tasks on our behalf.

00:17:33.120 --> 00:17:35.540
So I'll go ahead and
use the front-end Docker

00:17:35.540 --> 00:17:38.610
file for the front-end image
and the voting service Docker

00:17:38.610 --> 00:17:40.640
