00:26:47.668 --> 00:26:50.210
And instead of inheriting the
build settings from the default

00:26:50.210 --> 00:26:52.310
profile, we'll go
ahead and change it

00:26:52.310 --> 00:26:53.775
to Google Cloud Build.

00:26:53.775 --> 00:26:55.820
And now, as you can
see, the project IDE

00:26:55.820 --> 00:26:58.970
has been auto-populated with
my default Google Cloud project

00:26:58.970 --> 00:27:00.710
IDE.

00:27:00.710 --> 00:27:03.350
Let's continue to inherit
the deployment settings

00:27:03.350 --> 00:27:05.270
from the default profile,
which is currently

00:27:05.270 --> 00:27:08.180
configured to use kubectl.

00:27:08.180 --> 00:27:10.580
If you need more
customization options,

00:27:10.580 --> 00:27:14.980
you can directly work with
the scaffold.yaml file here.

00:27:14.980 --> 00:27:18.460
I'll go ahead and press Save
and proceed with the deployment.

00:27:18.460 --> 00:27:22.690
And we'll use the GKE profile,
and we'll use the cluster

00:27:22.690 --> 00:27:24.610
that Sarah has just started.

00:27:24.610 --> 00:27:27.342
And now, it's prompting
us for the image registry.

00:27:27.342 --> 00:27:29.050
Because we are deploying
this application

00:27:29.050 --> 00:27:31.090
to a remote cluster,
we also need

00:27:31.090 --> 00:27:33.160
to push the images to
a registry which is

00:27:33.160 --> 00:27:36.350
accessible from that cluster.

00:27:36.350 --> 00:27:38.830
And it's currently
auto-populated with my default

00:27:38.830 --> 00:27:40.540
gcr.registry.

00:27:40.540 --> 00:27:42.280
Let's go ahead and use it.

00:27:42.280 --> 00:27:45.122
And now, the
deployment has started.

00:27:45.122 --> 00:27:46.580
When the deployment
is in progress,

00:27:46.580 --> 00:27:48.380
I would like to bring
to your attention

00:27:48.380 --> 00:27:51.170
a number of things that are
happening behind the scenes.

00:27:51.170 --> 00:27:54.950
The Cloud Code deploy command
is building all the Docker files

00:27:54.950 --> 00:27:57.830
in the application using
Google Cloud Build,

00:27:57.830 --> 00:28:01.250
tagging them, and pushing
them to the remote registry--

00:28:01.250 --> 00:28:03.060
in this case, gcr.io.

00:28:03.060 --> 00:28:05.060
And then it will update
all the Kubernetes

00:28:05.060 --> 00:28:08.160
manifest in the application
with a new image path,

00:28:08.160 --> 00:28:11.030
then uses kubectl to
apply the Kubernetes

00:28:11.030 --> 00:28:13.540
manifest to the remote cluster.

00:28:13.540 --> 00:28:17.320
After this, it will wait till
all the deployment resources

00:28:17.320 --> 00:28:19.600
are rolled out successfully.

00:28:19.600 --> 00:28:21.727
It then waits till
all the load balancers

00:28:21.727 --> 00:28:23.560
and the ingress resources
in the application

00:28:23.560 --> 00:28:25.420
are assigned an IP address.

00:28:25.420 --> 00:28:28.700
And finally, if you pull all
the publicly-exposed endpoints

00:28:28.700 --> 00:28:32.135
in the application, I'll
display them right here

00:28:32.135 --> 00:28:33.010
in the output window.

00:28:35.740 --> 00:28:40.910
Now, it's currently waiting
for the ingress resource.

00:28:40.910 --> 00:28:42.910
Let's wait for it for
a few more seconds.

00:28:51.121 --> 00:28:52.580
Awesome.

00:28:52.580 --> 00:28:54.353
So now, let's use the link here.

00:28:54.353 --> 00:28:56.020
And now, we are
browsing the application

00:28:56.020 --> 00:28:59.420
deployed to the GKE cluster.

00:28:59.420 --> 00:29:01.016
Let's submit a vote.

00:29:01.016 --> 00:29:02.823
And awesome, it's working.

00:29:02.823 --> 00:29:03.740
SARAH D'ANGELO: Great.

